since leaping into the world of  serverless critter junction's been  reaching new limits  but i've got a few tricks to take it up  to the next level  stay tuned  a core promise of serverless compute  platforms like cloud run and cloud  functions is that you don't need to  worry about your infrastructure  your service skills automatically  that works great when your whole stack  auto scales but what if your service  depends on api  or databases with rate or connection  limits  that's what happened with critter  junction's new companion app codenamed  the wallet app  it allows users to send or receive  virtual currency  over a marketplace every time a user  sends or receives cash  the change the user wall data in the  database kicks off a cloud function  to return a confirmation cloud function  is a  lightweight serverless compute solution  that lets you  create single purpose and standalone  functions that respond to cloud events  and because serverless compute products  are stateless  they use cloud sql a fully managed  relational database service for stateful  data like user wallet amounts  after the wallet app gained popularity  because of youtube live streamers  cloud sql's maximum connection limit was  reached  and that meant the upstream functions  weren't able to provide  new wall amount confirmations  so my team came in to give them a few  tips to scale the wallet app  and any future serverless workloads they  plan to deploy  let's review how serverless functions  scale as you take a function from your  local computer to the cloud a function  takes  inputs and provides an output response  that function can be repeated with many  inputs and provide  many outputs a serverless platform  manages the elastic horizontal scaling  of function  instances because google cloud can give  you near infinite scale  that can have consequences for other  systems that your function interacts  with  so what do you do well a lot of what i'm  about to tell you  involves applying limits to operations  to protect  underlying services and resources first  off you can configure  instant scaling limits to your functions  cloud functions offer max  instant settings this feature limits how  many concurrent instances  of your functions are running and  attempting to establish database  connections  so if your database can only handle 100  concurrent connections  you might set your max instances to a  lower value  say 75 since each instance of a function  can only handle  a single request at a time this means  you can only handle 75 concurrent  requests  at a given time saving your database for  maxing out  at its rate limit even during a spike  second use cloud tasks to limit the rate  of work done  sometimes the limit you're worried about  isn't the number of concurrent  connections but the rate at which  work is performed for example  imagine you need a call to an external  api that has a limited  per minute quota cloudtask is a fully  managed service that lets you manage the  execution  dispatch and delivery of distributed  tasks  you can manage the way work gets done  and perform the work outside of the  serverless handlers  in work queues cloudtask supports  rate and concurrency limits so  regardless of its rate at which work  arrives it's performed with rates  applied  for example if large influx of api  requests come in  cloud tasks can buffer the rate at which  your request can be handled by the api  third use stateful storage to defer the  results from long running operations  sometimes you want your functions to be  able to defer work that might take a  while  but still give a response to the caller  that the task was received you want to  send the results of the task to the  caller  eventually also known as long-running  operations  for example it might not make sense to  encode a large video file  inside a serverless instance you could  use cloud tasks  if the caller of your workload only  needs to know that the request was  submitted  but if you want the caller to be able to  retrieve a status or eventual result  you need an additional stateful system  to track that job there are several ways  you can do this using a combination of  cloud functions  cloud pub sub and firestore while  critter junction has just started to  explore serverless with cloud native  apps like the layout and wallet apps  they're constantly revisiting how to  improve performance and scalability  like using mac serverless instances  cloud tasks to limit the rate of work  and stateful storage for long running  operations  for more details on these tips and an  awesome decision tree check out the link  below  and stay tuned for our next serverless  scaling tips and remember  always be architecting  [Music]  you
 

